rn-media: run a client hook on the prefetch path (hook `on_prefetch_load`)

WHY THIS EXISTS
    mpv's `--prefetch-playlist` opens the next playlist entry while the
    current one is still playing, and it opens the entry's RAW filename:
    prefetch_next() (player/loadfile.c) calls start_open() directly and never
    touches the hook pipeline.  process_hooks() has exactly six call sites in
    that file — on_before_start_file, on_load, on_load_fail, on_preloaded,
    on_unload, on_after_end_file — and all six are inside play_current_file()
    or the terminate path.  None is reachable from prefetch_next().

    So a client that resolves URLs in `on_load` (custom scheme -> signed CDN
    URL, plex-transcode:// -> HLS, our own resolver) never sees the prefetched
    entry, and the prefetch reaches the stream layer with an unresolvable URL.

    Worse than "no hook": the prefetch is then THROWN AWAY at the boundary.
    open_demux_reentrant() (loadfile.c:1218-1246 in 0.41.0) decides whether
    the in-flight open can be reused by a byte-exact strcmp of mpctx->open_url
    (the raw filename) against mpctx->stream_open_filename (the post-on_load,
    rewritten value).  For any resolver that actually rewrites, that compare
    fails, cancel_open() runs — mp_thread_join() on the doomed opener thread,
    on the core thread, AT the transition — and a cold synchronous open
    follows.  `prefetch-playlist=yes` plus a URL-rewriting resolver is
    therefore measurably WORSE than prefetch off, which is the opposite of
    what the option is for.

    Upstream documents this as intentional and permanent.  DOCS/man/options.rst
    (v0.41.0, lines 4312-4313, under --prefetch-playlist at 4307):

        This does not work with URLs resolved by the ``youtube-dl`` wrapper,
        and it won't.

    ytdl_hook.lua is an `on_load` hook, so that sentence is upstream's refusal
    of exactly this feature.  There is no option, no build flag and no client
    API that turns it on: verified against every mpv release from v0.33.0
    through v0.41.0 and current master — prefetch_next() is byte-identical in
    v0.40.0, v0.41.0 and master, and no version in that range ever calls
    process_hooks().  A fork patch is the only route.

    rn-media needs it because our queue is a network queue: gapless playback
    across a CDN boundary is what `prefetch-playlist` buys us (measured on
    device: 25 ms handover with it, 644 ms and an underrun without), and
    resolved/expiring URLs are what a real audio app has.  Without this patch
    those two features are mutually exclusive.

PROVENANCE
    The idea and the tap point — run the hook pipeline inside prefetch_next()
    and re-read stream-open-filename afterwards — follow
    ales-drnz/libmpv-scripts (BSD-3-Clause), `patches/mpv/shared/
    patch_prefetch_hook.py`, the patch set behind the Flutter package
    `mpv_audio_kit`.  That is the only shipped precedent for hooks on mpv's
    prefetch path, and it targets mpv 0.41.0 exactly.

    This implementation is written from scratch and differs from it in five
    deliberate ways, each fixing a defect found while reviewing it against the
    0.41.0 source.  They are listed under "WHAT WE DO DIFFERENTLY" below
    because they are the reason this file exists rather than a vendored copy.

APPLIES TO
    mpv 0.41.0.  New at this version; there is no earlier rn-media revision of
    this patch.

WHAT IT ADDS
    hook `on_prefetch_load`
                    Fired from prefetch_next() immediately before the
                    prefetch open, once per prefetched entry.  Register with
                    the ordinary client API (mpv_hook_add / MPV_EVENT_HOOK /
                    mpv_hook_continue) — no new export, no ABI change; the
                    export set of the shipped binary is unchanged (55 names,
                    see include/mpv.ver).

                    While it is open, `stream-open-filename` reads the
                    prefetched entry's URL and may be WRITTEN to resolve it.
                    Everything else — `path`, `duration`, every
                    demuxer-derived property, `playlist-current-pos` — still
                    describes the CURRENTLY PLAYING track, which is still
                    playing.

    `prefetch-playlist-entry-id`
                    int64, read-only.  playlist_entry.id of the entry being
                    prefetched.  M_PROPERTY_UNAVAILABLE outside the hook,
                    because outside the hook the value would be stale.  This
                    is the hook's missing payload: mpv_event_hook carries only
                    {name, id}, MPV_EVENT_START_FILE has NOT been sent for a
                    prefetched entry, and playlist-current-pos still points at
                    the playing track — so without this property the only key
                    a resolver has for the entry it is being asked about is the
                    URL string it was handed.

    Presence in a shipped artifact is proved by the string
    "[rn-media] prefetch hook resolved: " (player/loadfile.c), which nothing
    else in the tree emits; buildscripts/rn-media-release.sh asserts it
    against the stripped .so.

HOW IT WORKS
    prefetch_next() snapshots the next entry's url / stream_flags / id, points
    mpctx->stream_open_filename at its own copy of the url, sets
    mpctx->in_prefetch_hook, runs process_hooks("on_prefetch_load"), reads the
    (possibly rewritten) value back, restores the field, and calls start_open()
    with whatever came out.

    If the client rewrote the URL, the opener now works on the RESOLVED url —
    which is also what the play-time `on_load` will produce for the same entry,
    so open_demux_reentrant()'s strcmp succeeds at the boundary and the
    prefetched demuxer is actually reused.  That reuse is the entire point.

    If the client did not rewrite, or no client registered the hook at all,
    the function does what unpatched mpv does, with the same arguments.

THREADING AND COST
    Core thread only, from the single call site handle_update_cache() ->
    prefetch_next().  process_hooks() is a blocking pump (mp_hook_start, then
    mp_idle() until completion), so a client holds the core for as long as it
    takes to answer — no decode, no AO refill, audio continues only out of the
    AO queue (order 0.2-0.8 s on our measured device).  That budget is the
    client's problem, and it is a HARDER problem here than at `on_load`,
    because prefetch fires mid-track over live audio rather than between
    tracks: handle_update_cache() calls prefetch_next() on the first poll
    after the current file is fully read, which for a normal audio file is
    seconds after playback started, not seconds before the boundary.  A client
    that cannot answer from cache synchronously must continue the hook
    unchanged and resolve later.

    With no client registered the added cost is one mp_hook_exists() call — a
    walk of the (usually empty) hook array — and nothing else.  The URL is
    borrowed from the playlist entry and only copied inside the hook block, so
    that path allocates nothing, swaps nothing, and hands start_open() exactly
    the arguments upstream hands it.  (mp_hook_start() on an unregistered name
    would already be harmless — run_next_hook_handler() finds no match and
    mp_hook_test_completion() then returns true without ever entering the
    mp_idle() loop — but it still walks the list and wakes the core, and it
    would force the copy.)

WHAT WE DO DIFFERENTLY (and why)
    1. A distinct hook name, `on_prefetch_load`, not `on_load`.
       The prior art fires `on_load` and does not remove the play-time
       process_hooks(mpctx, "on_load") at loadfile.c:1725, so the same
       semantic hook fires twice per entry with nothing in the payload to tell
       the passes apart.  They are genuinely different phases: this one runs
       mid-track and the entry may never play (the queue can be edited, and
       upstream's own manual disclaims prefetch correctness when it is).  A
       separate name lets a client resolve at prefetch time and answer the
       play-time on_load from cache — which it MUST do, because if the second
       pass returns a different string (a fresh nonce, a new signature) the
       strcmp fails and the prefetch is discarded after all.

    2. No NULL-mask of mpctx->demuxer.
       mp_property_stream_open_filename()'s setter refuses writes while a
       demuxer is attached (player/command.c).  At `on_load` time that is
       always NULL (play_current_file() asserts it); during prefetch the
       PLAYING track's demuxer is very much alive, so the prior art sets
       mpctx->demuxer = NULL around the hook and restores it after.
       That is a ~50-site blast radius: `grep -c "mpctx->demuxer"
       player/command.c` is 50, and for the length of the hook every
       demuxer-derived property of the playing track — duration, file-size,
       seekable, is-network, metadata, chapters, demuxer-cache-*, track-list —
       reads as unavailable to any client that happens to look.  Our event
       thread and media-session layer read exactly those.  It also silently
       suppresses underrun detection for the playing track, because
       handle_update_cache() calls clear_underruns() BEFORE its
       `if (!mpctx->demuxer) return;` — i.e. the dropout this design risks
       becomes invisible to mpv's own buffering logic for precisely the window
       in which it is most likely.
       Instead: one bool on MPContext, one extra condition in that one setter.
       The other 49 sites are untouched and the playing track's state is not
       modified at all.

    3. The re-entry guard lives on MPContext, not in a file-static.
       A guard is required: process_hooks() -> mp_idle() ->
       handle_update_cache() -> prefetch_next() is a real cycle, and
       mpctx->open_active is still false at that point (start_open() has not
       run yet), so the existing early-return does not break it.  The prior
       art uses a function-scope `static bool`, which is process-global.  It
       happens to be safe for the recursion it guards, but rn-media runs
       several mpv cores in one process by design, so the honest scope is the
       core.  We reuse in_prefetch_hook itself — it is set for exactly the
       window that needs guarding.

    4. The entry's identity is exposed (`prefetch-playlist-entry-id`).
       See "WHAT IT ADDS".  The prior art keys its resolver table off the URL
       string, which is all that is available to it — and mp_normalize_path()
       in the property GETTER makes that key fragile for anything that is not
       a URL (a relative local path comes back absolute).

    5. Nothing reads the playlist entry after the hook.
       url, stream_flags and id are snapshotted BEFORE process_hooks(), and the
       entry pointer is nulled at that point.  The prior art reads
       new_entry->stream_flags AFTER the hook has returned; a client that runs
       playlist-remove while the hook is open frees that entry
       (common/playlist.c: playlist_remove() -> playlist_entry_unref(), which
       deallocates at reserved == 0), so that read is a use-after-free
       reachable from the client API.
       We also re-check mpctx->stop_play and mpctx->open_active after the
       hook, since the core kept servicing commands throughout.

    Not changed, and worth stating: the setter does not fire
    mp_notify_property() during the prefetch hook.  The swap is reverted before
    the hook returns, so the playing track's stream-open-filename never
    actually changed; notifying would leave every observer of that property
    holding the prefetch URL permanently, because the restore is a plain
    assignment (and so is play_current_file()'s own at loadfile.c:1686).

REGRESSION TEST
    tests/prefetch_hook_test.c, driven by tests/run.sh.  It builds a NATIVE
    libmpv from these same patched sources and asserts all three cases — no
    client registered, a client that continues unchanged, a client that
    rewrites — plus the re-entry guard and the absence of a property-change
    notification for the prefetch-time write.  Device-free, ~15 s.  It is a
    real regression test and not a demo: delete the `in_prefetch_hook` early
    return and the run FAILS in about 3 seconds with 9 failed assertions,
    "exactly one on_prefetch_load" first among them.  (The harness caps the
    runaway at 21 invocations and stops; an uncapped one reached 138 nested
    invocations of the hook for a single entry within one 25 s window.)

REBASING
    Four files, no build-system files.  The anchors to re-check after a
    version bump are:
      player/loadfile.c   prefetch_next()'s body — the whole function is
                          rewritten by this patch.  Byte-identical upstream in
                          v0.40.0, v0.41.0 and master; the last real change
                          was v0.39 -> v0.40, which moved the
                          `!mpctx->open_active` test into the early return.
                          Also depends on process_hooks() still being a
                          blocking pump and on start_open() still copying its
                          `url` argument (talloc_strdup at loadfile.c:1202) —
                          if it ever borrowed it instead, the talloc_free()
                          after the call becomes a use-after-free.
      player/core.h       struct MPContext, the block around
                          `char *stream_open_filename;`.
      player/command.c    mp_hook_start()'s body (mp_hook_exists() goes
                          immediately after it), the M_PROPERTY_SET arm of
                          mp_property_stream_open_filename(), and the
                          {"stream-open-filename", ...} property-table row,
                          which the new row follows.
      player/command.h    the mp_hook_* declaration block.
    Independent of patch 004 (audio/out/*, common/global.h, and command.c's
    AUDIO property region ~1900 and the {"audio-out-params", ...} row) and of
    002/003 (include/mpv/client.h, player/client.c, meson.build, sub/*).  The
    two command.c patches touch different regions of the same file and apply
    in numeric order.
    If upstream ever adds hooks to the prefetch path itself, drop this patch
    and keep only the property — but check the name it chose first.

diff --git a/player/loadfile.c b/player/loadfile.c
--- a/player/loadfile.c
+++ b/player/loadfile.c
@@ -1278,11 +1278,93 @@
     if (!mpctx->opts->prefetch_open || mpctx->open_active)
         return;
 
+    // rn-media: re-entry guard. The hook below pumps mp_idle(), which calls
+    // handle_update_cache(), which is the one caller of this function — and
+    // mpctx->open_active is still false at that point, so the test above does
+    // not catch it. On MPContext rather than in a file-static because one
+    // process runs several mpv cores.
+    if (mpctx->in_prefetch_hook)
+        return;
+
     struct playlist_entry *new_entry = mp_next_file(mpctx, +1, false, false);
-    if (new_entry && new_entry->filename) {
-        MP_VERBOSE(mpctx, "Prefetching: %s\n", new_entry->filename);
-        start_open(mpctx, new_entry->filename, new_entry->stream_flags, true);
+    if (!new_entry || !new_entry->filename)
+        return;
+
+    MP_VERBOSE(mpctx, "Prefetching: %s\n", new_entry->filename);
+
+    // rn-media: `url` is borrowed from the entry until the hook block takes a
+    // copy, so with no hook registered nothing is allocated and the start_open()
+    // at the bottom receives exactly what upstream passes it.
+    char *url = new_entry->filename;
+    int stream_flags = new_entry->stream_flags;
+    char *owned = NULL;         // our copy of the URL; NULL unless we hook
+    bool resolved = false;
+
+    // rn-media: upstream never runs hooks here (DOCS/man/options.rst on
+    // --prefetch-playlist: "This does not work with URLs resolved by the
+    // youtube-dl wrapper, and it won't"), so a client that resolves URLs in
+    // on_load sees the prefetch reach the stream layer unresolved — and the
+    // prefetch is then thrown away at the boundary anyway, because
+    // open_demux_reentrant() compares mpctx->open_url against the post-on_load
+    // stream_open_filename byte-for-byte. A separate hook name and not
+    // "on_load": these are different phases (this one runs mid-track, and the
+    // entry may never play), and reusing the name would fire the same semantic
+    // hook twice per entry with no way for the client to tell them apart.
+    if (mp_hook_exists(mpctx, "on_prefetch_load") &&
+        mpctx->playing && mpctx->stream_open_filename)
+    {
+        // Snapshot HERE and stop looking at the entry: the hook re-enters the
+        // core, so a client can playlist-remove it and free it while the hook
+        // is open (common/playlist.c: playlist_remove -> playlist_entry_unref).
+        // `owned` is a talloc ROOT, because the stream-open-filename setter
+        // allocates the rewritten string as a child of whatever the field
+        // points at.
+        uint64_t entry_id = new_entry->id;
+        owned = talloc_strdup(NULL, new_entry->filename);
+        url = owned;
+        new_entry = NULL;
+
+        char *saved = mpctx->stream_open_filename;
+        // Only this one field is swapped. mpctx->filename, mpctx->playing and
+        // mpctx->demuxer keep describing the PLAYING track, which is still
+        // playing and is what a client reading `path` or `duration` means.
+        mpctx->stream_open_filename = owned;
+        mpctx->prefetch_entry_id = entry_id;
+        mpctx->in_prefetch_hook = true;
+
+        process_hooks(mpctx, "on_prefetch_load");
+
+        // Read back before the restore: the setter made the rewritten string a
+        // child of `owned`, so it dies with the talloc_free() below.
+        resolved = strcmp(mpctx->stream_open_filename, owned) != 0;
+        if (resolved) {
+            char *new_url = talloc_strdup(NULL, mpctx->stream_open_filename);
+            talloc_free(owned);
+            owned = url = new_url;
+        }
+
+        mpctx->in_prefetch_hook = false;
+        mpctx->prefetch_entry_id = 0;
+        mpctx->stream_open_filename = saved;
+
+        // A hook blocks the core for as long as the client wants it to, and the
+        // core kept servicing commands while it did: playback may be ending,
+        // and a load command may already have claimed the opener thread.
+        if (mpctx->stop_play || mpctx->open_active) {
+            talloc_free(owned);
+            return;
+        }
+
+        if (resolved)
+            MP_VERBOSE(mpctx, "[rn-media] prefetch hook resolved: %s\n", url);
     }
+
+    // Unresolved, this is exactly what upstream does, with the same arguments.
+    // Resolved, the URL handed to the opener is the one the play-time on_load
+    // will also produce, which is what makes open_demux_reentrant()'s strcmp
+    // succeed and the prefetched demuxer actually get reused.
+    start_open(mpctx, url, stream_flags, true);
+    talloc_free(owned);         // ta_free() is a no-op on NULL
 }
 
